  <title>4.6.5: Interpreting Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00937</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.6.5: Interpreting Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3578fe5d-104e-4255-9999-2875cb4de1b9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>A squirrel runs up and down a tree.</para>
<para>The graph shows the height of the squirrel, \(h\) (feet), as a function of time, \(t\) (seconds).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Piecewise graph. Horizontal axis, 0 to 8, time, seconds. Vertical axis, 0 to 16, height, feet. Graph starts at 0 comma 9, increases, deceases to 4 comma 0, increases, then flattens out at 6 comma 10."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/50aa7651b1fe19a899593f61b9abfcf0b087a126"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the highest point the squirrel reaches?</item>
   <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> 14 feet</para>
   <item>Solve \(h(t)=0\). What does this solution tell you about the squirrel?</item>
   <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(t=4\) means that after 4 seconds, the squirrel is at the bottom of the tree on the ground.</para>
  <item>Find the vertical intercept of the graph. What does it tell you about the squirrel?</item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \((0,9)\) means that at the beginning, the squirrel is 9 feet high in the tree.</para>
</list>

<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title> More Chances</title>
<para>Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool-down, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool-down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para></section>
</content>
